+# table_export
+
+Export the exact rating tables of the model as Python data.
+
+`table_export` returns what [`tables`](tables.md) returns, already parsed: for the same
+arguments, `model.table_export(X) == json.loads(model.tables(X))`, with the same values and
+key order. The document is parsed once, and not written out again, so use this method when
+your code works with the tables rather than storing or sending the JSON text.
+
+--8<-- "_snippets/feature-matching-note.md"
+
+## Method call format {#call-format}
+
+```python
+table_export(X,
+             ref_measure=None,
+             laplace=1.0,
+             basis_json=None,
+             measure_floor=None,
+             overflow=None,
+             sample_weight=None,
+             exposure=None)
+```
+
+## Parameters {#parameters}
+
+The same as [`tables`](tables.md#parameters).
+
+## Return value {#output-format}
+
+A `dict`. For a regression or binary classification model, the document with the intercept
+(`f0`), the dense tables (`tables`) and the factored effects (`factored`). For a
+multiclassification model, a dict keyed by class label, with one such document per class. See
+[Rating tables](../../model-analysis/rating-tables.md) for the format.
+
+## Usage examples {#usage-examples}
+
+```python
+tables = model.table_export(train_data)
+print(tables["f0"])  # the intercept
+for table in tables["tables"]:
+    print(table["feature_names"], table["shape"])
+```
